NAME
       ls - list directory contents (also dir and vdir)

SYNOPSIS
       ls [OPTION...] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       ls lists the entries of each directory given (the current one by
       default) sorted by name, and shows other FILEs by name. Names that
       start with a dot are hidden unless -a or -A is given.

   The long format (-l)
           -rw-r-----. 1 student devops 2048 Mar  2 09:15 report.txt
           drwxrws---+ 2 root    devops   26 Mar  2 09:20 project

       1. The type (- file, d directory, l symbolic link, b and c devices,
          p pipe, s socket) and the permissions for owner, group and others
          (r read, w write, x execute; s and t are the special bits, see
          chmod).
       2. A . after the permissions means the file has an SELinux context; a
          + means it has an ACL as well (see getfacl).
       3. The number of hard links.
       4. The owner and the group.
       5. The size in bytes (for a directory, the size of its list of names).
       6. The time of the last change of the contents.
       7. The name; for a symbolic link, also -> and where it points.

       vdir is the same as ls -l, and dir is the same as ls.

OPTIONS
       -l     The long format.

       -a, --all
              Show hidden entries too, including . and ..

       -A, --almost-all
              Show hidden entries, without . and ..

       -d, --directory
              Show a directory itself, not what is in it (ls -ld /srv).

       -h, --human-readable
              With -l, sizes such as 4.0K and 2.1M.

       -R, --recursive
              List subdirectories too, all the way down.

       -t     Sort by time, newest first.

       -S     Sort by size, biggest first.

       -r, --reverse
              Reverse the sort order (ls -ltr puts the newest last).

       -i, --inode
              Show the inode number (equal for hard links).

       -Z, --context
              Show the SELinux context of each file.

       -n, --numeric-uid-gid
              Like -l, with UID and GID numbers instead of names.

       -F, --classify
              Add / after directories, * after programs, @ after links.

       -1     One name per line.

       -L, --dereference
              Show the target of symbolic links instead of the links.

       --full-time
              With -l, the full date and time.

       --color=WHEN
              Colour the names: always, auto or never.

EXAMPLES
       Everything in the home directory, with details:

           ls -la ~

       The permissions of a directory itself:

           ls -ld /srv/project

       The SELinux context of the web pages:

           ls -Z /var/www/html

       The newest files in /var/log last:

           ls -ltr /var/log

       Check that two names are hard links (same inode):

           ls -li /root/data.txt /root/data-link.txt

SEE ALSO
       stat(1), chmod(1), getfacl(1), find(1), tree(1)
